on abelian groups. See also Li [5] for a related asymptotic improvement on Nikiforov’s
bound.
  In order to attack Problem 1.1, Leonida and Li [4, Conjecture 4.2] formulated a weighted
analogue. Let Sn denote the set of real symmetric n × n matrices with entries in [0, 1].
Conjecture 1.2 (Leonida–Li [4]). One has
                                                         n
                                        inf λn−1 (M ) ≥ − .
                                       M ∈Sn             3
   The main result of this paper is a complete solution to Conjecture 1.2. In fact, we prove
a slightly stronger statement, allowing arbitrary nonnegative diagonal entries.
Theorem 1.3. Let n ≥ 2, and let A = (aij ) be a real symmetric matrix of order n with
eigenvalues
                               µ1 ≥ µ2 ≥ · · · ≥ µn .
Assume that
                       0 ≤ aij ≤ 1      (i ̸= j),      aii ≥ 0    (1 ≤ i ≤ n).
Then
                                                           2n
                                         µn−1 + µn ≥ −        .
                                                            3
In particular, µn−1 ≥ − n3 .

  Combining Theorem 1.3 with the identity A(G) + A(G) = J − I and Weyl’s inequality,
we obtain the following graph-theoretic consequence. In particular, it yields a stronger
conclusion than Problem 1.1.
Theorem 1.4. Let G be a graph on n ≥ 3 vertices. Then
                                          n
                                 λ3 (G) ≤ − 1.
                                          3
Remark 1.5. The bound in Theorem 1.4 is sharp whenever 3 | n. Indeed,
                                                  n
                          λ3 Kn/3 ∪ Kn/3 ∪ Kn/3 = − 1.
                                                     3
More generally, Leonida and Li [4, Theorem 2.2] constructed a family of graphs Ha,b of
order 3(a + b) satisfying
                                                 |V (Ha,b )|
                               λ3 (Ha,b ) = a + b − 1 =      − 1.
                                                     3
These provide nonregular equality examples, and the closed blow-ups of this family still
have third-eigenvalue proportion tending to 1/3.
  The paper is organised as follows. In Section 2 we prove Theorem 1.3. In Section 3 we
deduce Theorem 1.4.

