                 3. Deduction of the graph-theoretic theorem
Proof of Theorem 1.4. Let G be the complement of G. Then
                                   A(G) + A(G) = J − I,
where J is the all-ones matrix. The eigenvalues of J − I are n − 1 and −1 with multiplicity
n − 1. In particular,
                                     λ2 (J − I) = −1.
  We apply Weyl’s inequality for Hermitian matrices in the form
                  λi (X) + λj (Y ) ≤ λi+j−n (X + Y )              (i + j ≥ n + 1),
6                                                 Q. TANG

see, for example, [3, §4.3]. Taking
                        X = A(G),          Y = A(G),         i = 3,       j = n − 1,
we obtain
                                  λ3 (G) + λn−1 (G) ≤ λ2 (J − I) = −1.
By Corollary 2.2,
                                                          n
                                              λn−1 (G) ≥ − .
                                                          3
Hence
                                                                   n  n
                              λ3 (G) ≤ −1 − λn−1 (G) ≤ −1 +          = − 1.
                                                                   3  3
This proves the claimed bound.                                                                           □
Remark 3.1. Theorem 1.4 is sharp for all n divisible by 3. Determining the exact maxi-
mum of λ3 (G) for 3 ∤ n remains an interesting problem.

